1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,820
Saturday, seven a.m.

2
00:00:01,820 --> 00:00:02,880
The harbour.

3
00:00:02,880 --> 00:00:04,260
Ravi said to meet someone here.

4
00:00:04,740 --> 00:00:05,360
A ship is unloading.

5
00:00:05,640 --> 00:00:07,580
The crane lifts container after container.

6
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:10,680
Then you notice one container isn't hanging from

7
00:00:10,680 --> 00:00:11,060
the crane.

8
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,420
It's walking.

9
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:14,620
Underneath it, holding it over her head like

10
00:00:14,620 --> 00:00:16,900
a backpack, is a girl in overalls.

11
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:17,760
She puts it down.

12
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:18,660
The ground shakes.

13
00:00:19,340 --> 00:00:20,080
Hi, she says.

14
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:20,620
I'm Bo.

15
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:23,120
So what if you were super strong?

16
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,240
Not gym strong.

17
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:25,440
Container strong.

18
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,540
Let's start with the strongest humans alive.

19
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:32,460
In 2020, an Icelandic strongman named Hafthor Bjornsson

20
00:00:32,460 --> 00:00:34,460
deadlifted five hundred and one kilograms.

21
00:00:35,020 --> 00:00:36,240
That's about seven adults.

22
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:39,500
An empty shipping container weighs over two tonnes.

23
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:42,080
A full one can weigh more than twenty.

24
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:43,460
Bo's one was full.

25
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:47,120
If you want real super strength, look down.

26
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:50,780
In 2010, scientists tested a tiny dung beetle

27
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:52,700
that can pull more than a thousand times

28
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:53,460
its own body weight.

29
00:00:53,460 --> 00:00:56,580
For you, that would be like pulling eighty

30
00:00:56,580 --> 00:00:57,580
tonnes.

31
00:00:57,580 --> 00:00:58,200
But here's the catch.

32
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:00,740
Small things are strong for their size.

33
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:02,240
Big things aren't.

34
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,020
It's called the square-cube law.

35
00:01:05,460 --> 00:01:07,400
Galileo worked it out almost four hundred years

36
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:07,700
ago.

37
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:10,440
Make something twice as big, and its strength

38
00:01:10,440 --> 00:01:11,440
goes up four times.

39
00:01:11,880 --> 00:01:14,000
But its weight goes up eight times.

40
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:15,920
That's why a beetle the size of a

41
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:16,840
person would collapse.

42
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:18,920
And why a real giant would be crushed

43
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:19,880
by their own weight.

44
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:23,700
So Bo's muscles are clearly not normal.

45
00:01:23,840 --> 00:01:24,780
But what about her bones?

46
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:26,760
Your thigh bone is surprisingly tough.

47
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:28,540
Pushed straight down, it can hold something like

48
00:01:28,540 --> 00:01:29,740
thirty times your body weight.

49
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:32,160
But a full container weighs about as much

50
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:33,100
as three hundred people.

51
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:34,900
Your thigh bones would snap like breadsticks.

52
00:01:35,500 --> 00:01:37,380
And all that weight lands on two small

53
00:01:37,380 --> 00:01:37,740
shoes.

54
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:39,420
You'd sink into the ground like it was

55
00:01:39,420 --> 00:01:39,740
mud.

56
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:41,880
So Bo's power isn't just muscle.

57
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:44,060
Her bones, her skin, even the ground under

58
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:45,500
her feet would have to play along.

59
00:01:45,500 --> 00:01:47,660
Bo lets you try.

60
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:49,520
She hands you a shopping trolley.

61
00:01:49,900 --> 00:01:50,640
Then a motorbike.

62
00:01:51,080 --> 00:01:53,500
Then she lifts a car with one hand

63
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:55,800
and tells you to stand underneath it, just

64
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:56,320
for a photo.

65
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:57,960
You smile for the photo.

66
00:01:58,600 --> 00:01:59,840
Your knees are shaking.

67
00:02:01,320 --> 00:02:03,040
That night, all five of you sit on

68
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:03,760
top of a container.

69
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:06,520
Kai, Nia, Ravi, Bo, and you.

70
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:09,400
The star that isn't a star is bigger

71
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:09,700
now.

72
00:02:10,060 --> 00:02:10,800
It has a tail.

73
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:12,420
Kai finally says it.

74
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:13,440
It's not a star.

75
00:02:13,880 --> 00:02:14,300
It's a rock.

76
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:15,740
And it's coming here.

77
00:02:15,740 --> 00:02:17,140
Bo cracks her knuckles.

78
00:02:17,980 --> 00:02:18,820
Then we catch it.

79
00:02:19,680 --> 00:02:21,440
So what's the first thing you'd lift?

80
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:22,400
Tell us in the comments.

81
00:02:22,920 --> 00:02:25,160
And subscribe, because next time, the group tries

82
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:27,400
to stop a rock from space, and finds

83
00:02:27,400 --> 00:02:28,800
out what that would really take.
